course: dictionary
lesson: 14017
title: Magnetised
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Magnetised comes from magnet. To magnetise something means to make it into a magnet, so it can pull objects made of iron or steel. «Sam stroked the steel needle with a bar magnet until it was magnetised.» Stroking means moving the magnet along the needle, so the needle has been made into a magnet.
explain: Magnetised can also describe a thing that is already a magnet. «The magnetised needle swung round and pointed north.» The needle now has its own north and south pole, so it turns to line up with the Earth's magnetism. Magnetise is the verb, magnetises and magnetised show when it happens, and magnetising means doing it now.
explain: Use the context to work out the meaning. In «Sam stroked the needle with a magnet, and then it picked up a paper clip», the paper clip tells you the needle has become a magnet. Swap your meaning into the sentence: «the needle was made into a magnet» still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Magnetised means made into a magnet.
fact: A magnetised object can pull iron and steel.
fact: A magnetised object has its own north and south pole.
fact: «Magnetised» is the past form of «magnetise»: magnetise, magnetises, magnetised, magnetising.
fact: A magnetised steel needle floating on water makes a simple compass.
fact: Heating or dropping a magnet can make it lose its magnetism; this is called demagnetise.
exercise: «The nail stuck to the paper clip because the nail had been magnetised.» What does magnetised mean? | *Made into a magnet, so it can pull iron and steel | Painted bright red | Very heavy and cold | Made of soft wood
exercise: «Ben stroked the needle with a magnet until it was magnetised.» What can a magnetised object do? | Float and sing a song | Turn into wood | *Pull iron and steel | Glow brightly in the dark
exercise: «A magnetised needle floating on water pointed north.» What simple tool does this make? | A clock | *A compass | A kettle | A pair of scissors
test: Which word is the past form of magnetise? | *magnetised | magnetising | magnetise | magnets
test: Heating or dropping a magnet can make it do what? | Turn a bright colour | *Lose its magnetism | Make a loud noise | Grow bigger
exam: What does demagnetise mean? | Weigh a magnet on scales | Paint a magnet silver | Make something into a magnet | *Make a magnet lose its magnetism
